00:00:05.952 --> 00:00:08.880
QUAN TO: So I want to thank
you guys for all coming today.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.536
I appreciate you guys taking the
time to come out and see this.

00:00:11.536 --> 00:00:13.160
And today, we're
actually going to talk

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.169
about the Private Catalog.

00:00:15.169 --> 00:00:17.210
So this is actually the
first time we're actually

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:18.500
talking about this publicly.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.530
We just launched
our alpha last week.

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:24.430
And so I want to talk to
you about what we've built

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:27.427
and why this might be
beneficial for your enterprise.

00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:29.885
So before we start, I actually
want to take a quick survey.

00:00:29.885 --> 00:00:32.710
How many of you here actually
consider yourselves a cloud

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:35.390
IT admin?

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:35.890
Cool.

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:37.014
All right, we've got a few.

00:00:37.014 --> 00:00:38.592
How about developers?

00:00:38.592 --> 00:00:39.860
A few here.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.510
Architects?

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:45.330
One, two, three, four.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:46.290
OK, cool.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:46.790
All right.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:49.252
So this talk is actually going
to touch on most of that.

00:00:49.252 --> 00:00:50.960
For the rest of you
here, hopefully we'll

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:52.970
have something for
you guys, as well.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:56.630
But basically, we'll
talk about what we're

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:59.430
doing with the Private Catalog.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.340
So I'm going to define the
Private Catalog the way

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.800
we think it should be.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.250
It's to enable service
producers and cloud

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:12.430
admins to control
distribution of solutions.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:16.310
We want to ensure internal
compliance and governance.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.450
And we want solutions
to be discoverable

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.680
within your enterprise-- and,
of course, easily deployable.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.400
So the words control,
compliance, governance,

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.577
discoverable--

00:01:27.577 --> 00:01:29.910
so those are the key things
that I want you to take away

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.440
from this session today.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.240
And those are the
pillars that we

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.490
used in order to actually
build the Private Catalog.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.295
So I'm going to do things
a little different today.

00:01:40.295 --> 00:01:42.840
Instead of giving you a bunch
of slides of how great this is

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.090
and all the features and
functionality and everything,

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.259
I want to tell you a
little bit of a story.

00:01:47.259 --> 00:01:49.050
We're going to walk
through it and show you

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.420
how a fictional
enterprise might actually

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.910
leverage the Private Catalog.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:02.820
So there's a company
called ACME Game Studio.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.030
So ACME has about 500 people.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:07.660
They've been around
for a few years.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.930
They've got a huge market
share on game development

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.100
on mobile devices.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:14.670
So they develop for iOS.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.880
They develop for Android,
Windows Phone, Nintendo

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.080
devices--

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.670
anything you can think of.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:25.650
So these guys basically
have an IT environment

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:29.370
where they develop virtual
machines for their dev and test

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:30.750
environments.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.450
So the way it works
is, a developer at ACME

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.180
will go find the
right VM that they

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.510
need to develop their product
or their feature or their bug

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:40.490
fixes.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:41.650
They'll deploy that.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:45.170
That VM actually contains
all the SDK tools,

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.420
their build tools, integrations
with their build lab--

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:48.650
everything's there.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:53.040
So they can go ahead, fix
it, then deploy the test VM.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.344
The test VM's got to contain
all the emulators that they

00:02:57.344 --> 00:02:59.010
need in order to
actually test their bug

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:00.630
fixes or their features.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.700
And if everything
looks good, they'll

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.870
go ahead and check it in and
then shut down the machines.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:07.620
That way, they don't
spend a lot of money.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.050
They don't leave machines
idle all the time.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.460
And that's the environment
that they are actually using.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:19.050
So I want to introduce
Andy, who is a cloud admin.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.380
So Andy's the guy at
ACME that actually

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:23.810
manages those 20
virtual machines

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:25.195
within their enterprise.

00:03:27.494 --> 00:03:28.910
So he's the one
that updates them.

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:32.540
He notifies all the developers
when everything is changed.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:35.324
And so there's new tools
coming out all the time.

00:03:35.324 --> 00:03:36.990
There's patches coming
out all the time.

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.380
So he's updating constantly--
like, every few weeks,

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:40.670
he's changing things.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:42.860
And originally, he was
sending emails to everyone

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.490
when things changed.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.190
And so emails get lost.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:47.840
He sends a lot emails.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.630
As you guys know, developers
then end up ignoring those.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:53.835
And then they get lost, and no
one knows what's going on next.

00:03:53.835 --> 00:03:55.460
So he handles a lot
of support tickets.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.200
He gets 30 to 40 a week.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.140
And most of these
tickets are like,

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:00.360
"Where's the latest image?"

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:01.820
Just, "I want to get
my job done," just,

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:03.005
"Where's my latest image?"

00:04:03.005 --> 00:04:05.360
So he decided he's
going to create a wiki.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:08.144
So anytime he made the
update, he updates the wiki.

00:04:08.144 --> 00:04:09.560
He tells everyone,
go to the wiki.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.018
The wiki's going to have a link
to the latest and greatest.

00:04:12.018 --> 00:04:12.890
Everyone was happy.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:15.960
Then he went on vacation.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.589
The guys taking over
forgot to update the wiki.

00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:20.420
People started
deploying older images,

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:24.630
and then they ran into the
same problems over and over.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.552
So here's Darryl.

00:04:26.552 --> 00:04:27.760
Darryl's a developer at ACME.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:28.980
He's been there for
a couple of years.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:29.760
He loves the company.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:30.635
He loves the culture.

00:04:30.635 --> 00:04:31.680
He just hates the tools.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.420
He has no idea
where things are at.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.420
He just wants to deploy
the latest and greatest.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:37.920
He wants to be in
compliance with whatever

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:38.910
the company's set.

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:41.320
All the rules, all
the governance stuff--

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:43.110
he just doesn't want
all that friction.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:45.450
He just wants to
get his job done.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.820
And so whenever he
needs a new image,

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.120
he has no idea where to get it.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.870
There's a bunch of
emails floating around.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:51.510
There was a wiki.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:52.510
There's no wiki anymore.

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:53.860
He doesn't know what's going on.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.100
He has to ask around, ask
people, ask coworkers,

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.450
phone a friend.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:59.395
He just doesn't know
what he needs to do.

00:04:59.395 --> 00:05:02.620
So Darryl basically
just doesn't care.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:05.369
He just launches his VMs,
goes, fixes his bugs,

00:05:05.369 --> 00:05:06.660
and then just keeps it running.

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:10.200
So he's constantly
causing a lot of money

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.030
to be wasted while
his VMs are actually

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.880
just being left around.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.640
And because the tools
keep going updated a lot,

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:19.380
anytime he makes a
change, he checks it

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:21.390
in-- he's making a
huge risk that he's

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:22.650
going to break the build.

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:24.441
So when he does break
the build, then he'll

00:05:24.441 --> 00:05:28.200
go find the right VM, go code
for his change, go test it,

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.720
and then check that in,
causing a couple of delays

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.210
for everyone else, blocking
everyone in the pipe.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:36.199
So not really great.

00:05:36.199 --> 00:05:37.865
So does this sound
familiar to you guys?

00:05:37.865 --> 00:05:39.110
Do you guys handle this?

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:40.330
I see a lot of nodding heads.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:43.100
Good.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.500
So let's go try to
help Andy fix this.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:47.667
So I'm going to actually
do a demo of the live bits.

00:05:47.667 --> 00:05:50.041
I mentioned that we actually
shipped the alpha last week.

00:05:50.041 --> 00:05:51.512
So I'll be running
the real thing

00:05:51.512 --> 00:05:53.720
and showing you guys how
Andy's going to actually fix

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:54.678
some of these problems.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:02.860
Can you guys switch over?

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:03.490
Cool.

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:05.790
All right.

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:10.060
So I'm actually logged
in as the ADME acmin--

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:12.720
sorry, the ACME admin.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:16.390
And to get to the Private
Catalog, on the left nav

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:18.180
was the Private Catalog.

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:21.510
So what you're seeing here is
what we call the zero state.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:22.920
So you've got nothing in here.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:24.110
This is the Private Catalog.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:25.860
There's nothing for
you to see, because we

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.390
haven't added anything.

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:29.910
As an admin, I actually
have a button here

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:32.340
that says Go to Catalog
Manager-- so that's on the left

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:35.890
here, Manage Solutions.

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:39.000
So I'm actually now
in the Admin Console

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.825
for the Private Catalog.

00:06:40.825 --> 00:06:43.200
So the first thing I want to
do is actually go and create

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:44.350
a catalog.

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:45.240
So what is a catalog?

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:50.190
A catalog is really just a
grouping of the solutions

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:53.290
that you want to make
available to your end users.

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:55.550
So creating a catalog
is actually really easy.

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:57.000
Just click on Create Catalog.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:01.250
And let's just call
this Dev Tools--

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:04.490
tools for developers.

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:06.034
So the name is
actually important,

00:07:06.034 --> 00:07:07.700
because that's actually
what we're going

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:10.320
to show to your end users.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:14.810
And then the description is
just for the admin itself.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:16.850
So I'm actually creating
a catalog right now.

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:18.650
It's taking a few
seconds, because this is

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:21.200
real code working in the back.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:23.540
And the visibility of
catalogs is actually

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:27.950
dependent on a few things.

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:30.072
I'm actually going
to back up, because I

00:07:30.072 --> 00:07:32.030
want to show you how
multiple catalogs interact

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:32.850
with each other.

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.010
So let me actually create
one more catalog here.

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:36.985
I'll just call this Test Tools.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:44.080
So as this is being created,
I mentioned visibility

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:45.320
depends on a few things.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:47.620
One is, when you
create a catalog,

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:50.580
after you're ready to make
it visible to your end users,

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.950
you want to share it somewhere
in the GCP hierarchy.

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:56.270
And that will be at the org
level, the folder level,

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:58.150
or the project level.

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:00.325
Then you need to grant
access to your users

00:08:00.325 --> 00:08:04.090
to that org, folder, or project.

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:08.579
And that user will also need
an IAM role to view catalogs.

00:08:08.579 --> 00:08:10.870
So those things all need to
be in effect before the end

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:11.800
users can see it.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:16.060
So as the admin, you control
where it's getting displayed

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:19.270
and then who has access to it.

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:21.760
So I've created my two catalogs.

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:24.880
So there's a Dev Tools
and a Test Tools.

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:28.760
And going in there,
I can add a solution.

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:29.740
So we're in alpha.

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:31.820
We still have a long ways to go.

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:37.000
But for our alpha, we have two
solution types that we support.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:39.360
The first is a link solution.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:41.650
And a link solution is
really just a reference link

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:42.620
to anything.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.130
It could be a link to
a Terraform script.

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:47.730
It could be a link
to documentation.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:49.480
It could be a link to
a GitHub repository.

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:53.440
So it's really whatever
you feel should

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:54.910
be available to your end users.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:56.590
You can add that.

00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:58.787
The other one is a
Deployment Manager solution.

00:08:58.787 --> 00:09:01.120
So for those that don't know
what Deployment Manager is,

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:04.150
Deployment Manager is
basically a way for you

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:09.160
to set up a directive to
deploy GCP first-party content.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:11.170
And so you can use
Deployment Manager

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:14.490
to deploy VMs,
containers, deploy

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:17.230
a VM with an auto-scaling
group and a load balancer--

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:20.350
pretty much anything
that GCP can support.

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:21.430
So it's in my Dev Tools.

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:24.170
Let me go ahead and
create a product.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:28.090
So this is going to be
my Android Dev Tools--

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:36.024
so Android development
tools with SDK--

00:09:36.024 --> 00:09:36.940
I think they're at 29.

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:38.570
I'm not sure where they're at.

00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:40.760
The tag line is the
short description

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.370
that your end user's going to
see in the little tile view.

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:47.756
So I'll just call this
Android Dev Tools.

00:09:47.756 --> 00:09:49.910
So I can go ahead and browse.

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:51.370
Give me an icon here.

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:53.434
Here's one I've picked out.

00:09:53.434 --> 00:09:55.100
Support information
for your end users--

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:57.660
so if they ran into problems,
don't know who to contact.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:02.900
So I'll just say
contact admin@ACME.com.

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:05.360
ACME admin for the creator.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:06.860
So the Deployment
Manager template--

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:09.920
this is where you would go
ahead and point to the template.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:14.240
So let me show you what one
of these templates look like.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:16.400
So one I created,
which has a zip file.

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:17.770
I'm going to open it up.

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:23.870
And so on of the things I
mentioned earlier was control.

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:26.080
So how are you actually
going to control

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:27.570
what and where your
users are going

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:31.980
to deploy these solutions to?

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:33.010
So let me zoom in here.

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:40.480
And so in my example
here, I'm going

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:44.500
to show you how to actually
control what machine type

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:47.800
and what region the user
is going to deploy to.

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:50.190
And so here, we have zone--

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:52.220
the property zone.

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:55.362
And we've got machine type here.

00:10:55.362 --> 00:10:56.820
So this is actually
going to set up

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:58.625
a parameter as part of
the deployment experience

00:10:58.625 --> 00:11:00.390
so that when the end
user's deploying this

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:02.550
to their Private
Catalog, they'll actually

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:05.520
be able to select from a
list that you, as the admin,

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:09.142
curated, that you want
them to deploy to.

00:11:09.142 --> 00:11:11.100
And the way that works
is that there's a schema

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:15.000
file that you would include
with your Deployment Manager

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:17.645
template.

00:11:17.645 --> 00:11:19.930
I'm going to bring this up.

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:22.560
And so I mentioned how there
was machine type and zone.

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:24.180
So you'd define it
here, where you'd

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:26.090
have your different
machine types

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:27.910
and then the zones available.

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:30.600
So to show that this is
all real, let's go ahead

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:36.240
and actually just limit these
users to deploy to N1 standards

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:37.560
and deploy to just one region.

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:40.200
Let's say US Central.

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:43.350
So let me save
this and close it.

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:47.460
And I'm going to zip this
up, and then we'll actually

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:49.380
use this as part of the demo.

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:52.770
One gotcha is, you do need to
name your Deployment Manager

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:56.325
templates the same name as
the template file itself.

00:11:59.542 --> 00:12:01.000
We've got our DM
friends down here.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:02.090
They can explain why later.

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:02.798
I'm not sure why.

00:12:02.798 --> 00:12:06.010
But that's just a limitation
we have right now.

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:10.760
So I'm going to go ahead and
browse to that file I added.

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:13.180
And here's the zip file.

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:15.320
And I'm going to add.

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:16.670
So that's pretty much it.

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:19.860
That's how you add a solution
to the Private Catalog.

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:24.150
So to make it look a
little better later,

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:27.370
I'm going to go ahead and add
an iOS Dev Tools, as well.

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:34.470
And that way, at least
it will look pretty cool

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:37.020
when we actually have multiple
products in the catalog.

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:43.534
So here's the iOS tools.

00:12:43.534 --> 00:12:44.700
I can't use the other logos.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.460
And I've got another
zip file for iOS.

00:12:58.090 --> 00:13:00.270
Cool.

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:04.410
So now I've added two
solutions to one catalog

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:06.720
for my Private Catalog.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:09.810
And now, I'm going to
go to my test catalog.

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:12.430
And let me add a link solution.

00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:18.660
So let's just call this
the Android Developer

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:19.560
Documentation.

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:29.881
So Android Dev Docs.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:35.426
So I'm going to give
this an icon, here.

00:13:35.426 --> 00:13:39.300
Same support information,
contacts, admin--

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:40.960
admin.

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:43.390
So here, for your reference
links, you just put in a URL

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:44.180
to anything.

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:48.000
So in this case,
developer.android.com.

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:57.790
So now, I've added a link
solution to my test tools.

00:13:57.790 --> 00:14:02.400
So I have three solutions total.

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:04.500
So I mentioned
earlier, visibility

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:06.480
actually requires a few things.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:09.840
So it's sharing the catalog
somewhere in the GCP hierarchy,

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:12.420
granting permissions
to that user

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:14.130
to that location
in the hierarchy,

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:18.330
and making sure that end user
has the catalog viewer IAM

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:19.470
role.

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:23.080
So let me go ahead and share
this catalog to a project

00:14:23.080 --> 00:14:25.020
that I created earlier.

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:27.900
And I'm going to share the
second catalog to, also,

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:31.420
a project in my hierarchy.

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:33.150
Now I'm going to
go to the end user

00:14:33.150 --> 00:14:34.850
view of the Private Catalog.

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:37.350
So I'm at the org level, which
is why you don't see anything

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:38.290
right now.

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:40.740
So if I go to the project
level of where I just

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:43.680
shared those solutions
to, you can actually

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:47.610
see the three
solutions I created.

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:49.440
So now, let's go
into one of these.

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:50.220
Actually, no.

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:54.735
Before I go there, you can see
the different catalog names

00:14:54.735 --> 00:14:56.010
that I entered earlier.

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:57.540
So I mentioned
that was important,

00:14:57.540 --> 00:14:59.700
because this is what your
end user's going to see.

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:01.860
So I can filter by the catalogs.

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:03.740
And you can see the
two Deployment Manager

00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:05.100
templates I added there.

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:08.620
I can filter by the
different types.

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:10.595
So here's the Deployment
Manager templates.

00:15:10.595 --> 00:15:14.151
We've got the
reference link itself.

00:15:14.151 --> 00:15:16.985
I can also search
against the description,

00:15:16.985 --> 00:15:18.230
against the product name.

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:21.670
This will bring down-- it
will show me what's there.

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:25.260
And then if I go
into one of these,

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:28.212
this is the detail page
for the content I created.

00:15:28.212 --> 00:15:29.670
So you're probably
wondering-- this

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:32.220
looks a lot like the
Google Cloud Marketplace.

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:35.700
And it does, because we actually
are reusing the same components

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:37.445
on the front end
and the back end.

00:15:37.445 --> 00:15:39.100
In fact, we're
actually the same team

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:40.560
that built the
marketplace that's

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:42.460
building the Private Catalog.

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:44.610
So in here, I want
to go to a link.

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:48.567
If I click here, that will just
take me to the reference link.

00:15:48.567 --> 00:15:50.900
So if you're not deploying a
Deployment Manager template

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:54.040
and you want to use this, again,
you can point to documentation,

00:15:54.040 --> 00:15:57.530
point to GitHub repository,
point to Terraform scripts--

00:15:57.530 --> 00:15:59.900
whatever you'd like.

00:15:59.900 --> 00:16:05.860
And then on the tool side, I
can go ahead and actually launch

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:08.540
this VM that I
actually added earlier.

00:16:08.540 --> 00:16:13.930
So the first thing I need to do
is, here's my [INAUDIBLE] test.

00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:15.460
So here's the parameter.

00:16:15.460 --> 00:16:17.290
So you saw me, earlier,
actually delete

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:19.540
those items inside the zip file.

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:21.004
And so now, as an
end user, imagine

00:16:21.004 --> 00:16:22.170
that they're coming in here.

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:24.700
I want to deploy this
VM, and I can only

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:27.920
choose these machine
types and these zones.

00:16:27.920 --> 00:16:30.430
So your end users
can't go deploy

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:32.500
in the biggest possible,
beefiest machine

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:34.327
and waste a ton of
resources and money.

00:16:34.327 --> 00:16:36.160
And they can't deploy
two different regions,

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:38.870
because you're limiting
to what they can do here.

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:41.760
So I just go ahead and choose
the machine type and zone,

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:43.090
hit Deploy.

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:46.720
This goes through the regular
Deployment Manager experience.

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:49.330
And then this will just go
ahead and deploy and go through.

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:55.670
So I'm going to go back to
the slides, if you guys can

00:16:55.670 --> 00:16:58.235
help me switch back.

00:16:58.235 --> 00:17:01.490
All right.

00:17:01.490 --> 00:17:02.600
Cool.

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:05.089
So I just showed you how
easy it was to actually

00:17:05.089 --> 00:17:08.180
go create a catalog,
go add content to it,

00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:10.829
and then make it
visible to your users.

00:17:10.829 --> 00:17:14.432
So basically, from a
discoverability perspective,

00:17:14.432 --> 00:17:16.640
it's really easy for people
to know what's available,

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:19.480
because they just go to
this end user console.

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:23.060
For our governance and
compliance, you as the admin

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:25.099
make sure that your
users are deploying

00:17:25.099 --> 00:17:27.720
the latest and greatest that
you're making available.

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:29.390
And then from a
control perspective,

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:32.250
you saw how I basically
put in guardrails.

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:35.330
So those end users can't go
ahead and deploy willy-nilly

00:17:35.330 --> 00:17:38.190
on any machine types or
any regions that they want.

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:39.565
And because it's
all parametrized

00:17:39.565 --> 00:17:41.066
and it's through
Deployment Manager,

00:17:41.066 --> 00:17:42.440
you could change
those parameters

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:43.340
to wherever you want.

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:46.460
You can set up different
ranges for auto-scaling groups,

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:47.420
different parameters.

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:49.387
You can set up
different RAM types

00:17:49.387 --> 00:17:50.970
that you want them
to deploy, as well.

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:53.720
So all that configuration
and control is up to you

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:55.430
as the admin.

00:17:55.430 --> 00:17:57.220
Best of all, this
is going to be free.

00:17:57.220 --> 00:17:58.970
So we're not going to
charge you for this.

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:00.053
This is going to come out.

00:18:00.053 --> 00:18:01.910
You can just use this
in your enterprise.

00:18:01.910 --> 00:18:04.730
We want you to deploy
more GCP solutions,

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:08.400
but this is actually
going to be free.

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:10.670
So let's dig a little
bit into the four

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:11.840
pillars I mentioned earlier.

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:16.592
So discoverability-- so when you
have someone new to your team--

00:18:16.592 --> 00:18:18.050
someone that just
transferred over,

00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:19.790
someone new that you hired--

00:18:19.790 --> 00:18:22.040
I think it's great to just
say, go here and deploy all

00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:24.440
the approved solutions for you.

00:18:24.440 --> 00:18:25.880
This is a single tool.

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:27.950
So I've talked to
several customers

00:18:27.950 --> 00:18:30.207
in the last few months,
as we were building this.

00:18:30.207 --> 00:18:32.540
And I asked them explicitly,
what do you guys use today?

00:18:32.540 --> 00:18:34.540
How do people discover
stuff in your enterprise?

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:38.120
And I've heard everything
from wikis, emails,

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:42.320
notepad documents, spreadsheets,
word docs-- it's all over.

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:43.840
So we're getting
rid of all that.

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:45.910
Here's a single place.

00:18:45.910 --> 00:18:47.660
I showed the
searching, filtering--

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:50.540
so you can filter by
the solution type,

00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:52.340
by the different catalogs.

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:55.190
And then as an end
user, if you have access

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:58.550
to multiple catalogs,
you'll see a union

00:18:58.550 --> 00:19:01.040
of all that information--
which I kind of showed earlier,

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:04.160
where you had two catalogs,
and you actually saw everything

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:05.600
that I shared to that project.

00:19:08.650 --> 00:19:12.030
So for internal
compliance and governance,

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:13.510
this is all about
your end users.

00:19:13.510 --> 00:19:16.950
So your end users want to know
that they're always deploying

00:19:16.950 --> 00:19:17.970
the latest and greatest.

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:19.386
They don't want
to think about it.

00:19:19.386 --> 00:19:20.659
They don't want that friction.

00:19:20.659 --> 00:19:22.950
They want to be confident
they're deploying the latest,

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:27.180
best solutions that you, as
the IT admin, has put in there.

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:30.120
So they want to know that
they've deployed all the latest

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:31.445
security patches.

00:19:31.445 --> 00:19:32.820
Security's a big
thing right now.

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:34.110
There's new patches coming on.

00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:36.150
There's vulnerabilities
happening all the time.

00:19:36.150 --> 00:19:37.110
Images get updated.

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:39.693
They don't have to worry about
that, because you're putting up

00:19:39.693 --> 00:19:41.670
the latest and greatest.

00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:43.740
Every company has
its own policies.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:46.200
Again, they want to make sure
that wherever they deployed,

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:47.680
someone has approved,
so they don't

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:49.670
have to even think about it.

00:19:49.670 --> 00:19:51.690
And then for
compliance, if you have

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:54.870
a lot of VMs, a
lot of solutions,

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:57.540
and you need to
update them, we have

00:19:57.540 --> 00:19:59.850
APIs to allow you to
actually do bulk updates.

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:07.480
So control-- so sharing of the
catalogs at the GCP hierarchy,

00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:10.840
at the organization,
folder, or project level.

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:12.790
So visibility depends
on that end user

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:16.880
having access to that hierarchy
and having the IAM role.

00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:21.130
So talking about IAM roles, we
have an IAM role for admins,

00:20:21.130 --> 00:20:21.680
as well.

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:23.199
So you don't have a bottleneck.

00:20:23.199 --> 00:20:24.490
You don't just have one person.

00:20:24.490 --> 00:20:29.980
So you can have multiple admins
manage and curate your catalog

00:20:29.980 --> 00:20:33.020
and the products themselves.

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:34.870
So I mentioned earlier
the guardrails.

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:36.370
So you can put up
guardrails to make

00:20:36.370 --> 00:20:38.170
sure people aren't
deploying machines

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:39.760
that they shouldn't
be deploying to,

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:41.676
that they're actually
deploying to the regions

00:20:41.676 --> 00:20:43.360
that you're limiting them to.

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:46.780
You can add RAM, scaling
groups-- anything you want.

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:50.770
And then only the admins can add
content to the Private Catalog.

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:54.412
So admins are controlling
what actually gets to be there

00:20:54.412 --> 00:20:56.120
and what your end
users are going to see.

00:20:59.090 --> 00:21:01.660
So this highlights
that DM template again.

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:04.160
It's kind of hard to see on
that little notepad document I

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:05.059
had earlier.

00:21:05.059 --> 00:21:06.850
But what I've highlighted
here is basically

00:21:06.850 --> 00:21:10.480
how you would actually go
ahead and define the guardrails

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:12.900
within your Deployment
Manager template.

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:14.920
And then inside the
schema file, this

00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:18.370
is where you would enumerate
the different combinations

00:21:18.370 --> 00:21:20.860
of things that the users
would be able to get to.

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:26.110
So let's talk a little
bit about the APIs.

00:21:26.110 --> 00:21:28.191
So we built the APIs first.

00:21:28.191 --> 00:21:29.690
We build it with
the product itself.

00:21:29.690 --> 00:21:31.930
It wasn't an afterthought.

00:21:31.930 --> 00:21:35.350
And we do have admin APIs
for all the CRUD operations--

00:21:35.350 --> 00:21:38.500
so creation, update, deletion.

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:43.050
And so this is great for bulk
updating of your content.

00:21:43.050 --> 00:21:45.170
We have end user APIs as well.

00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:47.650
And so this is
allowing you to query

00:21:47.650 --> 00:21:50.620
for what your user
actually has access to.

00:21:50.620 --> 00:21:53.744
And so this will be useful for
enterprises that currently,

00:21:53.744 --> 00:21:55.660
potentially, have their
own solution right now

00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:58.210
and want to include
cloud software.

00:21:58.210 --> 00:22:00.990
Or let's say you had
ServiceNow in your enterprise,

00:22:00.990 --> 00:22:02.260
and you don't get rid of that.

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:04.570
But you do want
include GCP content.

00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:06.200
So you could use
the end user APIs

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:09.640
to check what the logged-in user
has access to and then query

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:11.120
for that.

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:15.010
And then for deployment, you can
leverage the Deployment Manager

00:22:15.010 --> 00:22:16.900
APIs, which are
currently public.

00:22:16.900 --> 00:22:19.100
And these slides will
be available later.

00:22:19.100 --> 00:22:21.690
So there's a link to the
Deployment Manager APIs.

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:26.390
So end user query APIs--

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:29.180
we've got catalogs, where you
can list and get catalogs.

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:30.320
You can do products.

00:22:30.320 --> 00:22:33.200
You can do versions.

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:35.539
On the admin side,
we've got catalogs.

00:22:35.539 --> 00:22:36.955
So you can go
create the catalogs,

00:22:36.955 --> 00:22:40.340
you can update catalogs,
you can delete catalogs.

00:22:40.340 --> 00:22:42.680
I think we have an
un-delete catalog, as well,

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:44.360
if you screw up.

00:22:44.360 --> 00:22:46.260
So we have identities
so you can check

00:22:46.260 --> 00:22:50.120
what the identity of the user is
and what roles that they have.

00:22:50.120 --> 00:22:51.260
We have association.

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:52.950
This is what I called
sharing, earlier.

00:22:52.950 --> 00:22:58.160
So this is APIs to share
the catalog with some part

00:22:58.160 --> 00:23:00.500
of the GCP hierarchy.

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:03.050
So then, of course, the
products and version

00:23:03.050 --> 00:23:05.241
APIs, as well, for
your solutions.

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:12.750
So I want to give you an example
of how you can use these APIs.

00:23:12.750 --> 00:23:16.680
So let's say you actually
had a CI/CD solution

00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:18.300
to build these VMs.

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:19.440
And so it exists today.

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:21.300
You've got, maybe,
a GitHub repository.

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:22.740
You've got some scripts.

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:25.260
And so any time someone
actually makes an update,

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:27.110
there's a process
that gets kicked off

00:23:27.110 --> 00:23:29.070
that basically
generates a new VM,

00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:32.160
deploys those scripts onto
the VM, runs those scripts.

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:35.490
And you can integrate with that
process that you currently have

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:38.940
and call the Private
Catalog APIs, which

00:23:38.940 --> 00:23:40.800
then will allow
you to then update

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:42.960
that solution inside
your Private Catalog,

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:43.950
for your end users.

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:46.020
That way, you've got
that full life cycle.

00:23:46.020 --> 00:23:47.170
Everything works.

00:23:47.170 --> 00:23:48.870
It's this full solution.

00:23:48.870 --> 00:23:51.360
And your users, again, don't
have to care about that.

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:52.860
They're always going
to be deploying

00:23:52.860 --> 00:23:53.860
the latest and greatest.

00:23:57.850 --> 00:24:00.430
So we launched our
alpha last week.

00:24:00.430 --> 00:24:02.690
This was actually a good sprint.

00:24:02.690 --> 00:24:05.360
We've actually worked on it
for only about six months.

00:24:05.360 --> 00:24:07.250
So what do we have today?

00:24:07.250 --> 00:24:10.930
So we have that UX experience
that you saw for the end user

00:24:10.930 --> 00:24:13.000
and for the admin.

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:14.560
So we have our APIs
to where you can

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:18.570
do CRUD operations on the
products and on the catalog.

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:21.940
We've got APIs for end
users, for any integrations

00:24:21.940 --> 00:24:25.600
that you may want into
your own homegrown solution

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:29.140
or something like a ServiceNow
or anything out there.

00:24:29.140 --> 00:24:31.840
We allow you to create
multiple catalogs.

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:34.420
So it's up to you on
how you want to define

00:24:34.420 --> 00:24:37.040
how your catalogs should work.

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:39.230
It could be a catalog
just for developers.

00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:41.510
It could be a catalog
for a certain platform.

00:24:41.510 --> 00:24:43.760
It could be a catalog just
for your legal department--

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:44.991
whatever you want.

00:24:44.991 --> 00:24:46.990
But you can create as
many catalogs as you want.

00:24:46.990 --> 00:24:49.550
There's no limits there.

00:24:49.550 --> 00:24:53.020
So you can have IAM roles
to allow other admins

00:24:53.020 --> 00:24:56.930
to go and manage those
catalogs and those solutions.

00:24:56.930 --> 00:24:59.350
So we support two
content types today.

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:01.600
So there's the reference
links and Deployment Manager

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:02.425
templates.

00:25:02.425 --> 00:25:05.290
And so our goal is, of course,
to continue adding to this

00:25:05.290 --> 00:25:08.660
and add more solution types.

00:25:08.660 --> 00:25:11.620
So there is a single point
of entry for your admins

00:25:11.620 --> 00:25:12.790
and your end users.

00:25:12.790 --> 00:25:15.490
And basically, the
IAM permissions

00:25:15.490 --> 00:25:18.760
will gate whether they have
access to the Admin Console

00:25:18.760 --> 00:25:19.750
or not.

00:25:19.750 --> 00:25:24.670
So that simplifies, so everyone
just goes to a single place.

00:25:24.670 --> 00:25:27.610
So we integrate the DM
experience, which looks exactly

00:25:27.610 --> 00:25:28.660
like the Cloud Launcher.

00:25:28.660 --> 00:25:30.130
So you have the same flows.

00:25:30.130 --> 00:25:32.734
So anyone familiar with
the Cloud Marketplace?

00:25:32.734 --> 00:25:34.150
We actually just
changed the name.

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:36.460
So anyone familiar with
the GCP Cloud Marketplace

00:25:36.460 --> 00:25:39.070
can just follow that same path.

00:25:39.070 --> 00:25:41.320
And then if you have
any org policies set

00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:44.397
within your organization and
if you use the DM constraints,

00:25:44.397 --> 00:25:45.730
we're going to respect all that.

00:25:45.730 --> 00:25:47.271
We're not going to
override anything.

00:25:47.271 --> 00:25:50.140
So all that works well
within the GCP environment.

00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:56.020
So I want to give you guys
an opportunity to influence

00:25:56.020 --> 00:25:57.230
our roadmap.

00:25:57.230 --> 00:25:59.530
So we have just
shipped an alpha.

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:02.692
And so we have a lot of ideas
on what we want to do next

00:26:02.692 --> 00:26:04.650
and how we want to make
this bigger and better.

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:06.108
But we want to make
it so that it's

00:26:06.108 --> 00:26:09.610
something that's enticing to
you, that you want to use,

00:26:09.610 --> 00:26:12.400
and that you're going to
deploy in your enterprise.

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:15.745
And so one way you can help
us and influence our roadmap

00:26:15.745 --> 00:26:18.910
is to actually go
take the survey.

00:26:18.910 --> 00:26:22.840
Actually, can you just
flip to the next--

00:26:22.840 --> 00:26:26.100
